<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Martí</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Martí was a Cuban poet, journalist and revolutionary, the national hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. Exiled for most of his life, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolutionary-party">Cuban Revolutionary Party</a> in 1892 to unite the exiles for a new war of independence. He was killed at Dos Ríos on 19 May 1895, soon after landing in Cuba, and his writings shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> modernismo and Cuban nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-marti.jpg" alt="José Martí" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cuba. Secretaría de Instrucción Pública y Bellas Artes. · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Mart%C3%AD_retrato_hecho_en_M%C3%A9xico_1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Julián Martí Pérez was born on 28 January 1853 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, to Spanish parents: a Valencian father, Mariano Martí Navarro, and Leonor Pérez Cabrera, a native of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. He was the older brother of seven sisters. When he was four the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and two years later they returned to the island. In January 1871 he sailed from Havana to Cádiz and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, where he studied law and took part in debate on the Cuban question through the Spanish press, circulating documents that protested Spanish actions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Martí lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> from 1875 and joined the editorial staff of the Revista Universal. In 1881, after a brief stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, he founded the Revista Venezolana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, which angered the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-guzman-blanco">Antonio Guzmán Blanco</a> and forced his return to New York. There he joined General Calixto García's committee of Cuban exiles and worked as a journalist for La Nación of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and Central American papers. He translated novels, served as consul for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, and founded the newspaper Patria, which became the organ of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolutionary-party">Cuban Revolutionary Party</a>. In a letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximo-gomez">Máximo Gómez</a> in 1882 he proposed a revolutionary party, to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from falling back on the Home Rule Party after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-zanjon">Pact of Zanjón</a>.</p><p>In 1891 he published the essay Nuestra América and, in October, the verses Versos Sencillos. In January 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> authorities stopped three ships at Fernandina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and confiscated weapons, ruining the Fernandina Plan. The uprising began on 24 February 1895, and a month later Martí and Gómez issued the Manifesto of Montecristi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Martí was killed fighting Spanish troops at the Battle of Dos Ríos, near the confluence of the Contramaestre and Cauto rivers, on 19 May 1895. Gómez had judged the Spanish position strong and ordered his men to disengage, but Martí, seeing a young courier, called for a charge. He wore a black jacket and rode a white horse. He was buried at Santa Ifigenia Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><p>He wrote poems, essays, a serialised novel, lectures, letters and a children's magazine, La Edad de Oro, and is counted a major figure of the Spanish American movement known as Modernismo. His prose influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruben-dario">Rubén Darío</a>. Verses from Versos Sencillos were adapted into the song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guantanamera">Guantanamera</a>. Martí opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, warned of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> designs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries should build institutions fitted to their own history and nature. His thought has been invoked in competing visions of the Cuban nation, and José Martí International Airport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Martí” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Guantanamera&quot;, Spanish for &quot;the woman from Guantánamo&quot;, is a Cuban patriotic song whose verses come from poems by the Cuban poet <b>José Martí</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/guantanamera">Guantanamera</a></li><li><blockquote><b>José Martí's</b> Cuban Revolutionary Party relaunched the war in 1895, and new constitutions at Jimaguayú in 1895 and La Yaya in 1897 revived it.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba-in-arms">Republic of Cuba in Arms</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. Died in Dos Rios, Cuba. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.14,-82.36&amp;year=1880" aria-label="Havana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2351 1310 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José Martí"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2801 1580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.14,-82.36&amp;year=1880">What was here in 1880? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
